{"a": 1}
```
{"b": 2} and Reply B: {"b": 2}. The rule requires for Reply A the fenced object {"a": 1}, because a yaml code fence is a code fence and it holds an object; for Reply B it requires the top-level object {"b": 2}, because no fence holds an object. The check's _FENCE regex recognizes only json, JSON, or bare fences, so it will ignore the yaml fence in Reply A, scan top-level objects, find both {"a": 1} and {"b": 2}, and return the last, {"b": 2}; for Reply B it returns {"b": 2}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check returned {"a": 1} for Reply A, showing it recognized the yaml fence, or if the rule were read to exclude non-json fences from "any fence".